      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think I agree with that idea, but what the semantics of `UsePerAppMemcg()` are currently supposed to be are not documented, so I can only look at how it\u0027s currently used. If we were to make that change, I think the function should be renamed (`DeviceWantsPerAppMemcg()`?)\n\nToday `UsePerAppMemcg` is used by platform code to gate per-app memcg behaviors. So it\u0027s currently \"broken\" for both v1 and v2 with invalid device configurations. This change fixes the condition so that per-app memcg behaviors are only attempted when they are actually possible instead of when they are requested but impossible.\n \nThe upside of exposing two functions is that callers can know about invalid device configurations before attempting to run code that depends on per-app actually being enabled.\n\nThe downside is that it\u0027s another function we\u0027ll have to maintain basically forever. And I guess callers would have to call two functions instead of one.\n\nSo I think the question is do callers actually need to know? No callers are checking for this today, despite the fact that the same problem has always existed with v1.\n\nMaybe what we should have instead is an interface to expose `descriptor.IsUsable()` information coupled with a specific cgroup, with prototypes similar to the task profiles functions that can accept combindations of UID, PID, and/or path. For memcg, basically `PerPIDMemcgEnabled()`, `PerUIDMemcgEnabled()` / `SysAppMemcgsEnabled()` type functions but for any controller, not named specifically for memcg.",
      "parentUuid": "e6ec679b_049292ee",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 178,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 202,
        "endChar": 15
      },
